      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "The austerity measures continue across the restaurant. Nagiyev confronts Kostya the bartender to deliver some bad news. Let's listen closely to their exchange in Russian first, and then walk through each line together.",
      "gap": 0.8
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Я просто делал свою работу.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "nagiyev",
      "t": "Мы экономим на всём.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "nagiyev",
      "t": "Мы сэкономили на бармене.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Вы не можете меня уволить.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "nagiyev",
      "t": "Ты уволен, балбес.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Балбес, да?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Это очень несправедливо.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "nagiyev",
      "t": "Ресторан в трудном положении.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Кто будет наливать напитки?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "nagiyev",
      "t": "Это больше не твоя проблема.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Хорошо, я уйду сегодня.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "nagiyev",
      "t": "Вот и отлично.",
      "gap": 0.9
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "Let us break down this interaction.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Я просто делал свою работу.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'I was just doing my job.' The verb for 'were doing' is in the masculine past tense, marked by that 'l' sound at the end.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "nagiyev",
      "t": "Мы экономим на всём.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'We are saving on everything.' This everyday Russian sentence pairs 'we' with 'everything'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "nagiyev",
      "t": "Мы сэкономили на бармене.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'We saved on the bartender.' Dmitry combines 'we' with 'bartender' in a natural Russian turn of phrase.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Вы не можете меня уволить.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'You cannot fire me.' This everyday Russian sentence pairs 'you' with 'to fire'. Then Dmitry responds.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "nagiyev",
      "t": "Ты уволен, балбес.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'You are fired, idiot.' Dmitry uses the informal singular 'you' here with 'blockhead', as friends and close colleagues do.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Балбес, да?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'An idiot, huh?' Notice how 'blockhead' sets up the clause and leads naturally into 'yes'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Это очень несправедливо.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'This is very unfair.' Kostya links 'this' directly with 'very much' to express the thought clearly.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "nagiyev",
      "t": "Ресторан в трудном положении.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'The restaurant is in a difficult situation.' Dmitry combines 'restaurant' with 'position' in a natural Russian turn of phrase. Then Kostya responds.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Кто будет наливать напитки?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Who will pour drinks?' This everyday Russian sentence pairs 'who' with 'drinks'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "nagiyev",
      "t": "Это больше не твоя проблема.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'This is no longer your problem.' This everyday Russian sentence pairs 'this' with 'problem'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Хорошо, я уйду сегодня.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Fine, I will leave today.' Notice how 'good' sets up the clause and leads naturally into 'today'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "nagiyev",
      "t": "Вот и отлично.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'That is excellent.' Dmitry links 'there' directly with 'and' to express the thought clearly. Now let us listen to the conversation again without interruption.",
